::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1; MLA.PC.VC.2
Question:
Which skill is needed to interpret complex numbers on the coordinate plane?
{
~A. Ignore coordinates#Coordinates matter in the complex plane.
=B. Use real and imaginary parts as coordinates#Correct. The real part is horizontal and the imaginary coefficient is vertical.
~C. Use only the real part#This ignores the imaginary coordinate.
~D. Use only the imaginary part#This ignores the real coordinate.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.3; MLA.PC.VC.4
Question:
Why can a complex number be represented in rectangular or polar form?
{
~A. It has no location#Complex numbers do have complex-plane locations.
~B. It is always real#Many complex numbers are not real.
~C. It has no angle#Polar form uses an angle.
=D. It has coordinate location and distance-direction information#Correct. Rectangular form uses coordinates, and polar form uses distance and direction.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.5
Question:
What happens to arguments when multiplying complex numbers in trigonometric form?
{
=A. They are added#Correct. Multiplication in trigonometric form multiplies moduli and adds arguments.
~B. They are multiplied#Moduli are multiplied, not arguments.
~C. They are ignored#Arguments are needed.
~D. They are subtracted every time#Subtraction is not the multiplication rule.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.1; MLA.PC.PA.2
Question:
Which conic-section idea is correct?
{
~A. A circle has no center#Circles have centers.
~B. A cone slice cannot form a circle#Cone slices can form conics, including circles.
=C. A circle can come from slicing a cone and is described by center and radius#Correct. Circles are conics and are described by center and radius.
~D. The radius is always r^2#The equation uses r^2, but radius is r.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.3; MLA.PC.PA.4
Question:
Which feature helps interpret a circle or parabola model?
{
~A. Only the answer choice letter#The answer letter is not mathematical evidence.
~B. No graph features#Graph features are important.
~C. Only the x-axis#The y-values and other features also matter.
=D. Key features such as center or vertex#Correct. Circle centers and parabola vertices help interpret models.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.5
Question:
What is the vertex used for in a parabola model?
{
=A. It identifies the turning point and helps interpret the graph#Correct. The vertex is a key graph and context feature.
~B. It gives the radius#Radius belongs to circles.
~C. It gives two branches#Two branches belong to hyperbolas.
~D. It is ignored in applications#The vertex is important in applications.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.6; MLA.PC.PA.7
Question:
Which features belong to ellipses?
{
~A. One radius only#That describes a circle.
~B. One directrix only#This is not the main ellipse feature set.
=C. Center, axes, vertices, co-vertices, and foci#Correct. These are key ellipse features.
~D. Two asymptotes only#Asymptotes belong to hyperbolas.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.8; MLA.PC.PA.9
Question:
What feature guides the branches of a hyperbola?
{
~A. Radius#Radius belongs to circles.
=B. Asymptotes#Correct. Asymptotes guide the branches of a hyperbola.
~C. Vertex only#The vertex alone is not enough.
~D. Modulus#Modulus belongs to complex-number polar form.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q09::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1; MLA.PC.VC.5; MLA.PC.PA.1
Question:
What is a common theme across complex numbers and conic sections?
{
=A. Representations connect algebra to geometry#Correct. Unit 5 connects equations and forms to geometric meaning.
~B. All graphs are circles#Only some graphs are circles.
~C. Angles are never used#Angles appear in polar and trigonometric form.
~D. Key features are optional#Key features are required for interpretation.
}
::PC_U05_PRETEST_Q10::
Question ID: PC_U05_PRETEST_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.2; MLA.PC.PA.4; MLA.PC.PA.6; MLA.PC.PA.8
Question:
Why should the equation form be identified before graphing a conic?
{
~A. It avoids all work#Work is still needed.
~B. It makes every graph the same#Conics have different forms and features.
=C. It determines the conic type and key features#Correct. Equation form tells which conic and features to use.
~D. It removes context#Context still matters in modeling problems.
}